{% set design %}
<section class="docs-section">
  <h2 id="d-usage">Usage</h2>
  <p>A dialog interrupts the flow to demand a decision or a short piece of
     input. It is the most expensive surface in an interface — everything on
     screen goes inert behind it — so it must be the only thing the user can
     act on while open.</p>
  <h3>Do use it for</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Destructive confirmations</strong> — "Delete conversation?"
        with Cancel and a danger action.</li>
    <li><strong>Short forms</strong> — rename, invite, move-to; a single
        <a href="text-fields.html">field</a> plus actions. Enter submits via
        <code>method="dialog"</code>.</li>
    <li><strong>Decisions that block the flow</strong> — unsaved-changes
        warnings before navigation.</li>
  </ul>
  <h3>Don't use it for</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Non-blocking feedback</strong> — "Message sent" is a
        snackbar, not a dialog; never make the user click an OK to dismiss
        good news.</li>
    <li><strong>Long or exploratory content</strong> — release notes,
        settings, browsing. That is the <a href="bottom-sheet.html">bottom
        sheet</a> or a normal page; dialogs cap at ~88vh for a reason.</li>
    <li><strong>Chaining decisions</strong> — a wizard behind three OKs is a
        page, badly compressed.</li>
  </ul>
  {{ callout({
    variant: "info",
    title: "The hard parts are platform work — by design",
    text: "showModal() provides the focus trap, the inert background, Escape-to-close and top-layer stacking natively. Flint deliberately ships no custom focus management: the classic hand-rolled overlay is 300 lines of fragile JS. flint.js only wires data-fl-open / data-fl-close and returns focus to the invoker on every close path. Open and close animations are pure CSS (@starting-style + allow-discrete).",
    href: "text-fields.html",
    linkText: "Field family"
  }) }}
</section>

